, II and III","d":"I and II only"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"National Emergency has no maximum prescribed period, while President''s Rule has a maximum of three years","e":"National Emergency can continue indefinitely with parliamentary approval every six months, whereas President''s Rule must end after a maximum of three years and normal constitutional machinery must be restored. See table below for the full comparison.","table":{"headers":["Basis of Comparison","National Emergency (Article 352)","President''s Rule (Article 356)"],"rows":[["Effect on state executive/legislature","Continue functioning; Centre gets concurrent powers","State executive dismissed; legislature suspended/dissolved"],["Law-making delegation","Parliament cannot delegate; must legislate itself","Parliament can delegate to President or specified authority"],["Maximum duration","None; can continue indefinitely","Three years maximum"],["Approval majority","Special majority","Simple majority"],["Effect on Fundamental Rights","Affects Fundamental Rights","No effect on Fundamental Rights"],["Revocation control","Lok Sabha can pass resolution for revocation","No such provision; only President can revoke"]]},"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"National Emergency affects only the state under emergency, while President''s Rule affects all states","b":"National Emergency has no maximum prescribed period, while President''s Rule has a maximum of three years","c":"Both require approval only by simple majority","d":"Both have a maximum prescribed period of three years"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"The financial stability or credit of India","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Public order","b":"National security","c":"The financial stability or credit of India","d":"Federal structure"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"38th Amendment Act","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"38th Amendment Act","b":"52nd Amendment Act","c":"42nd Amendment Act","d":"44th Amendment Act"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"44th Amendment Act","e":""