","November 14-26, 1949"]]},"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Eight","b":"Eleven","c":"Nine","d":"Ten"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Two years, 11 months and 18 days","e":"The Constituent Assembly held 11 sessions in all over this period.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Two years, 11 months and 18 days","b":"Two years and 6 months","c":"Three years and 2 months","d":"One year, 11 months and 18 days"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"60","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"30","b":"60","c":"75","d":"45"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"114 days","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"90 days","b":"114 days","c":"141 days","d":"165 days"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"₹64 lakh","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"₹75 lakh","b":"₹100 lakh","c":"₹50 lakh","d":"₹64 lakh"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"January 24, 1950","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"January 24, 1950","b":"November 26, 1949","c":"April 17, 1952","d":"January 26, 1950"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"The formation of new Parliament after the first general elections in 1951-52","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"November 26, 1949","b":"The Drafting Committee''s dissolution","c":"The adoption of the national flag","d":"The formation of new Parliament after the first general elections in 1951-52"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"Dr. B.R. Ambedkar","e":"The Constituent Assembly had 8 major committees. See table below for all major committees and their chairmen.","table":{"headers":["Committee","Chairman"],"rows":[["Union Powers Committee","Jawaharlal Nehru"],["Union Constitution Committee","Jawaharlal Nehru"],["Provincial Constitution Committee","Sardar Patel"],["Drafting Committee","Dr. B.R. Ambedkar"],["Advisory Committee on Fundamental Rights, Minorities and Tribal and Excluded Areas","Sardar Patel"],["Rules of Procedure Committee","Dr. Rajendra Prasad"],["States Committee (Committee for Negotiating with States)","Jawaharlal Nehru"],["Steering Committee","Dr. Rajendra Prasad"]]},"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Sardar Patel","b":"Alladi Krishnaswamy Ayyar","c":"Dr. B.R. Ambedkar","d":"Jawaharlal Nehru"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Five","e":"The five sub-committees were: Fundamental Rights (J.B. Kripalani), Minorities (H.C. Mukherjee), North-East Frontier Tribal Areas and Assam Excluded & Partially Excluded Areas (Gopinath Bardoloi), Excluded and Partially Excluded Areas other than Assam (A.V. Thakkar), and North-West Frontier Tribal Areas (Chairman not recorded in source).","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Five","b":"Three","c":"Four","d":"Six"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Drafting Committee","e":"The Drafting Committee was a major committee, chaired by Dr. B.R. Ambedkar. See table below for all minor committees and their chairmen.","table":{"headers":["Committee","Chairman"],"rows":[["Finance and Staff Committee","Dr. Rajendra Prasad"],["Credentials Committee","Alladi Krishnaswamy Ayyar"],["House Committee","B. Pattabhi Sitaramayya"],["Order of Business Committee","Dr. K.M. Munshi"],["Ad-hoc Committee on the National Flag","Dr. Rajendra Prasad"],["Committee on the Functions of the Constituent Assembly","G.V. Mavalankar"],["Ad-hoc Committee on the Supreme Court","S. Varadachari (Not an Assembly Member)"],["Committee on Chief Commissioners'' Provinces","B. Pattabhi Sitaramayya"],["Expert Committee on the Financial Provisions of the Union Constitution","Nalini Ranjan Sarkar (Not an Assembly Member)"],["Linguistic Provinces Commission","S.K. Dar (Not an Assembly Member)"],["Special Committee to Examine the Draft Constitution","Jawaharlal Nehru"],["Press Gallery Committee","Usha Nath Sen"],["Ad-hoc Committee on Citizenship","S. Varadachari (Not an Assembly Member)"]]},"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Credentials Committee","b":"House Committee","c":"Drafting Committee","d":"Press Gallery Committee"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"I and III only","e":"S. Varadachari was not a member of the Constituent Assembly, making II incorrect; he was brought in externally to chair this committee.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"I, II and III","b":"II and III only","c":"I and III only","d":"I and II only"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"August 29, 1947","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"August 29, 1947","b":"November 4, 1948","c":"December 13, 1946","d":"February 1948"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Seven","e":"Full membership of the Drafting Committee:","table":{"headers":["Member","Role/Note"],"rows":[["Dr. B.R. Ambedkar","Chairman"],["N. Gopalaswamy Ayyangar","Member"],["Alladi Krishnaswamy Ayyar","Member"],["Dr. K.M. Munshi","Member"],["Syed Mohammad Saadullah","Member"],["N. Madhava Rau","Replaced B.L. Mitter, who resigned due to ill-health"],["T.T. Krishnamachari","Replaced D.P. Khaitan, who died in 1948"]]},"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Eight","b":"Five","c":"Seven","d":"Six"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"February 1948","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"February 1948","b":"November 1948","c":"October 1948","d":"November 1947"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Eight months","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Four months","b":"Six months","c":"Eight months","d":"Twelve months"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"October 1948","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"October 1948","b":"June 1948","c":"November 1948","d":"February 1948"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"141 days","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"114 days","b":"189 days","c":"141 days","d":"165 days"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"November 4, 1948","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"November 4, 1948","b":"November 15, 1948","c":"November 14, 1949","d":"November 26, 1949"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Five days","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Five days","b":"Seven days","c":"Three days","d":"Ten days"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"November 15, 1948","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"November 15, 1948","b":"November 4, 1948","c":"November 14, 1949","d":"October 17, 1949"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"October 17, 1949","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"November 26, 1949","b":"November 9, 1948","c":"November 14, 1949","d":"October 17, 1949"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"I and III only","e":"Of the 7653 amendments proposed, only 2473 were actually discussed, not all of them, making II incorrect.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"II and III only","b":"I and III only","c":"I, II and III","d":"I and II only"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"November 14, 1949","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"November 26, 1949","b":"November 4, 1949","c":"October 17, 1949","d":"November 14, 1949"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"November 26, 1949","e":"This is also the date mentioned in the Preamble as when the people of India adopted, enacted, and gave themselves the Constitution. It is celebrated as Constitution Day (Samvidhan Divas), a designation made on November 19, 2015.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"January 24, 1950","b":"November 26, 1949","c":"January 26, 1950","d":"November 14, 1949"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"284","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"270","b":"284","c":"292","d":"299"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"395","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"395","b":"389","c":"448","d":"393"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"8","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"8","b":"10","c":"12","d":"9"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"It was enacted after the entire Constitution was already enacted","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"It was added through a later amendment","b":"It was enacted simultaneously with the rest of the Constitution","c":"It was enacted after the entire Constitution was already enacted","d":"It was enacted before the rest of the Constitution"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Law Minister","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"President of the Constituent Assembly","b":"Chairman of the Drafting Committee only","c":"Home Minister","d":"Law Minister"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"Father of the Constitution of India","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Father of Communal Electorate","b":"Iron Man of India","c":"Father of the Nation","d":"Father of the Constitution of India"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"November 19, 2015","e":"Constitution Day is also known as \"Samvidhan Divas.\"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"November 19, 2015","b":"January 26, 1950","c":"November 26, 2015","d":"November 26, 1949"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Articles 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 60, 324, 366, 367, 379, 380, 388, 391, 392 and 393","e":"The remaining, major part of the Constitution came into force on January 26, 1950 — the ''date of commencement,'' celebrated as Republic Day. See table below for what each of these Articles covers.","table":{"headers":["Article","Subject Matter"],"rows":[["5","Citizenship at the commencement of the Constitution"],["6","Rights of citizenship of certain persons who have migrated to India from Pakistan"],["7","Rights of citizenship of certain migrants to Pakistan"],["8","Rights of citizenship of certain persons of Indian origin residing outside India"],["9","Persons voluntarily acquiring citizenship of a foreign state not to be citizens"],["60","Oath or affirmation by the President"],["324","Superintendence, direction and control of elections to be vested in an Election Commission"],["366","Definitions"],["367","Interpretation"],["379","Provisions as to provisional Parliament and the Speaker and Deputy Speaker thereof (repealed)"],["380","Provisions as to President (repealed)"],["388","Provisions as to the filling of casual vacancies in the provisional Parliament and provisional Legislatures of the States (repealed)"],["391","Power of the President to amend the First and Fourth Schedules in certain contingencies (repealed)"],["392","Power of the President to remove difficulties"],["393","Short title"]]},"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Articles 1 to 10","b":"Articles 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 60, 324, 366, 367, 379, 380, 388, 391, 392 and 393","c":"Articles 5 to 9 only","d":"All 395 Articles"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Purna Swaraj Day, celebrated in 1930","e":"Purna Swaraj Day was celebrated on January 26, 1930, following the resolution of the Lahore Session (December 1929) of the INC.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Purna Swaraj Day, celebrated in 1930","b":"The Poona Pact","c":"The Lahore Session of the INC","d":"The signing of the Indian Independence Act"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Indian Independence Act 1947 and Government of India Act 1935","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Indian Independence Act 1947 and Government of India Act 1935","b":"Government of India Act 1935 and Regulating Act 1773","c":"Indian Independence Act 1947 only","d":"Charter Act 1833 and Indian Independence Act 1947"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Abolition of Privy Council Jurisdiction Act (1949)","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Indian Councils Act 1909","b":"Abolition of Privy Council Jurisdiction Act (1949)","c":"Charter Act 1853","d":"Government of India Act 1919"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Granville Austin","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"A.V. Dicey","b":"K.C. Wheare","c":"Granville Austin","d":"Ivor Jennings"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Naziruddin Ahmed","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Naziruddin Ahmed","b":"H.C. Mukherjee","c":"K.M. Munshi","d":"B. Pattabhi Sitaramayya"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Lord Viscount Simon","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Clement Atlee","b":"Lord Mountbatten","c":"Lord Viscount Simon","d":"Winston Churchill"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Winston Churchill","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Lord Viscount Simon","b":"Winston Churchill","c":"Lord Wavell","d":"Ramsay MacDonald"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Less than 4 months","e":"Full comparison of time taken by framers of other constitutions:","table":{"headers":["Country","No. of Articles","Duration (Dates)","Time Taken"],"rows":[["U.S.A.","7","May 25, 1787 to September 17, 1787","~4 months (115 days)"],["Canada","147","October 10, 1864 to March 1867","~2 years 5 months"],["Australia","128","March 1891 to July 9, 1900","~9 years 4 months"],["South Africa","153","October 1908 to September 20, 1909","~11 months"]]},"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"About 9 years","b":"About 2 years and 6 months","c":"1 year","d":"Less than 4 months"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"Elephant","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Lion","b":"Elephant","c":"Peacock","d":"Tiger"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Sir B.N. Rau","e":"See table below for other key roles and the individuals behind them.","table":{"headers":["Role","Person"],"rows":[["Constitutional advisor (Legal Advisor)","Sir B.N. Rau"],["Secretary to the Constituent Assembly","H.V.R. Iyengar"],["Chief draftsman of the Constitution","S.N. Mukherjee"],["Calligrapher of the original Constitution","Prem Behari Narain Raizada"],["Illuminated, beautified and ornamented the original Preamble","Beohar Rammanohar Sinha"],["Calligraphy of the Hindi version of the original Constitution","Vasant Krishan Vaidya"],["Decorated and illuminated the Hindi version''s calligraphy","Nand Lal Bose"]]},"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"H.V.R. Iyengar","b":"Sir B.N. Rau","c":"S.N. Mukherjee","d":"Prem Behari Narain Raizada"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Prem Behari Narain Raizada","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"S.N. Mukherjee","b":"Nand Lal Bose","c":"Prem Behari Narain Raizada","d":"Beohar Rammanohar Sinha"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"58th Amendment Act","e":"Enacted in 1987. Article 394-A was inserted in Part XXII, the last part of the Constitution.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"42nd Amendment Act","b":"44th Amendment Act","c":"58th Amendment Act","d":"61st Amendment Act"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"I and II only","e":"The Hindi translation is deemed authoritative for all purposes, but it is not stated to supersede the English text; both are to be construed as having the same meaning, making III incorrect.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"I and II only","b":"II and III only","c":"I and III only","d":"I, II and III"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"I, II and III","e":"Full list of Articles related to short title, commencement, Hindi text and repeals:","table":{"headers":["Article No.","Subject Matter"],"rows":[["393","Short title"],["394","Commencement"],["394A","Authoritative text in Hindi language"],["395","Repeals"]]},"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"I only","b":"I and II only","c":"II and III only","d":"I, II and III"},"answer":"d"}]') ON CONFLICT(chapter_slug) DO UPDATE SET subject_id=excluded.subject_id, chapter_number=excluded.chapter_number, title=excluded.title, answers_json=excluded.answers_json;
INSERT INTO chapter_content (chapter_slug, subject_id, chapter_number, title, answers_json) VALUES ('indian-polity-en-chapter-30-landmark-judgements-and-doctrines', 'indian-polity-en', 30, 'Landmark Judgements and Doctrines', '[{"a":"Madras","e":"The case is Romesh Thappar vs. State of Madras (1950), concerning Article 19(1)(a). As a sequel, the 1st Amendment Act (1951) added \"public order\" as a reasonable restriction under Article 19(2), a ground the Court had refused to accept in this judgement.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"West Bengal","b":"Madras","c":"Punjab","d":"Bombay"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Due process of law","e":"This narrow, textualist interpretation confined Article 21''s protection to arbitrary executive action alone, and held the field from 1950 until it was overruled in Maneka Gandhi (1978).","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Rule of law","b":"Judicial review","c":"Equal protection of law","d":"Due process of law"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"Section 14","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Section 14","b":"Section 22","c":"Section 8","d":"Section 3"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"15(1) and 29(2)","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"15(1) and 29(2)","b":"19(1) and 21","c":"14(1) and 16(2)","d":"25(1) and 26(2)"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Clause (4)","e":"Inserted by the 1st Amendment Act (1951), this clause effectively nullified the judgement''s effect.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Clause (3)","b":"Clause (4)","c":"Clause (6)","d":"Clause (2)"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Part III","e":"The Court ruled that the word \"law\" in Article 13(2) refers only to ordinary law and not constituent law made under Article 368, upholding the 1st Amendment Act (1951). This stance was reaffirmed in Sajjan Singh (1964) but overruled in Golak Nath (1967).","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Part IV","b":"Part XX","c":"Part XIV","d":"Part III"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"First Schedule","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"First Schedule","b":"Tenth Schedule","c":"Ninth Schedule","d":"Third Schedule"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"9th Amendment Act","e":"The transfer was carried out under the 1958 Indo-Pak Agreement (Nehru-Noon Agreement).","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"9th Amendment Act","b":"6th Amendment Act","c":"7th Amendment Act","d":"11th Amendment Act"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Jury system","e":"The system was fully removed only by the new Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973, though this case exposed its flaws.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Tribunal system","b":"Summary trial system","c":"Jury system","d":"Adversarial system"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Article 13(2)","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Article 245","b":"Article 368","c":"Article 12","d":"Article 13(2)"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"Doctrine of prospective overruling","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Doctrine of colourable legislation","b":"Doctrine of prospective overruling","c":"Doctrine of eclipse","d":"Doctrine of pith and substance"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"24th Amendment Act","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"26th Amendment Act","b":"25th Amendment Act","c":"24th Amendment Act","d":"22nd Amendment Act"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Doctrine of basic structure","e":"The Court overruled Golak Nath (1967) and upheld the 24th Amendment Act (1971), holding that Parliament can amend any provision including fundamental rights, except the basic structure.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Doctrine of harmonious construction","b":"Doctrine of basic structure","c":"Doctrine of severability","d":"Doctrine of pleasure"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"29th Amendment Act","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"31st Amendment Act","b":"42nd Amendment Act","c":"29th Amendment Act","d":"26th Amendment Act"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Article 329A","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Article 356","b":"Article 329A","c":"Article 71","d":"Article 105"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"39th Amendment Act","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"42nd Amendment Act","b":"38th Amendment Act","c":"40th Amendment Act","d":"39th Amendment Act"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"Maintenance of Internal Security Act (MISA), 1971","e":"The Court held that if Article 21''s enforcement is suspended under Article 359, a detenu has no locus standi to move a writ under Article 226. This ruling is no longer good law.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Maintenance of Internal Security Act (MISA), 1971","b":"Preventive Detention Act","c":"Unlawful Activities Prevention Act","d":"National Security Act"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"44th Amendment Act","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"43rd Amendment Act","b":"45th Amendment Act","c":"42nd Amendment Act","d":"44th Amendment Act"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"I and II only","e":"The Court actually held the right to \"live\" includes the right to live with human dignity, making statement (III) false.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"I, II and III","b":"I and III only","c":"II and III only","d":"I and II only"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"A.K. Gopalan case","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Shankari Prasad case","b":"A.K. Gopalan case","c":"Golak Nath case","d":"Kesavananda Bharati case"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Golden triangle rule","e":"The judgement also introduced the American concept of \"due process of law\" into Indian judicial interpretation, reversing the position taken in A.K. Gopalan.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Golden triangle rule","b":"Harmonious construction rule","c":"Doctrine of eclipse","d":"Basic structure rule"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Rarest of rare cases","e":"The Court upheld Section 302 IPC''s death penalty provision as not violative of Articles 19 or 21, but held it must be an exception rather than the rule.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Cases involving public officials","b":"Cases involving repeat offenders","c":"Cases with no eyewitnesses","d":"Rarest of rare cases"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"Article 31C","e":"The Court held that the limited amending power of Parliament, judicial review, and the harmony between fundamental rights and directive principles are all basic features of the Constitution.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Article 21","b":"Article 32","c":"Article 31C","d":"Article 14"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"24 April 1973","e":"This is the date on which the Kesavananda Bharati verdict was delivered; amendments to the Ninth Schedule before this date were held valid.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"25 June 1975","b":"6 September 1980","c":"26 January 1950","d":"24 April 1973"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"Section 125","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Section 340","b":"Section 125","c":"Section 197","d":"Section 482"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Muslim Women (Protection of Rights on Divorce) Act","e":"In the Danial Latifi case (2001), the Supreme Court upheld this Act''s validity but held a Muslim husband liable to pay maintenance even beyond the iddat period.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Muslim Personal Law (Shariat) Application Act","b":"Special Marriage Act","c":"Muslim Women (Protection of Rights on Divorce) Act","d":"Dissolution of Muslim Marriages Act"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"State Legislature","e":"The Court described repeated re-promulgation of ordinances as a \"fraud on the constitution.\"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Election Commission","b":"Governor''s Council","c":"State Legislature","d":"Parliament"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Absolute liability","e":"The Court held that compensation applications under Article 32 are maintainable for enforcement of the right to life under Article 21.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Absolute liability","b":"Comparative liability","c":"Vicarious liability","d":"Contributory liability"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Article 368(2)","e":"Paragraph 7 excluded the jurisdiction of the Supreme Court and High Courts without ratification by half the State Legislatures, as required for amendments affecting Article 368(2).","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Article 194","b":"Article 368(2)","c":"Article 356","d":"Article 105"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"I and III only","e":"The Court actually invalidated the 10% additional reservation order for economically backward sections, making statement (II) false.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"I and III only","b":"II and III only","c":"I only","d":"I, II and III"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"National Commission for Backward Classes","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Mandal Commission","b":"National Commission for Backward Classes","c":"National Reservation Authority","d":"Ram Nandan Commission"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Article 21","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Article 19","b":"Article 41","c":"Article 29","d":"Article 21"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"14 years","e":"Beyond this age, the state''s obligation to provide education was held to depend on its economic capacity. This case''s reasoning led to the 86th Amendment Act (2002), which inserted Article 21A.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"6 years","b":"14 years","c":"18 years","d":"21 years"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Concurrence","e":"The Court held that the senior-most judge of the Supreme Court should be appointed Chief Justice of India, overruling the S.P. Gupta case (1981), the First Judges case.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Concurrence","b":"Endorsement","c":"Recommendation","d":"Ratification"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Collegium system","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Collegium system","b":"Consultation system","c":"Memorandum system","d":"Concurrence system"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"I and II only","e":"The Court actually held that if the proclamation is held invalid, it is open for the court to restore the Legislative Assembly and Ministry, notwithstanding parliamentary approval — making statement (III) false.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"II and III only","b":"I and III only","c":"I, II and III","d":"I and II only"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"Secularism","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Secularism","b":"Federalism","c":"Rule of law","d":"Judicial review"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Articles 15 and 21","e":"The guidelines issued in this case, known as the Vishaka Guidelines, filled the legislative vacuum until the Sexual Harassment of Women at Workplace Act, 2013 was enacted.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Articles 14 and 19","b":"Articles 19 and 21","c":"Articles 15 and 21","d":"Articles 21 and 25"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Central Vigilance Commission","e":"This led to the enactment of the CVC Act, 2003. The judgement also struck down the Single Directive requiring prior government permission to investigate senior officers, though this was later reinstated by the Act.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Enforcement Directorate","b":"Central Bureau of Investigation","c":"Election Commission","d":"Central Vigilance Commission"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"Article 19(1)(a)","e":"The Court directed the Election Commission to make candidates furnish details of criminal cases, assets, liabilities, and educational qualifications.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Article 326","b":"Article 19(1)(a)","c":"Article 21","d":"Article 14"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"The State","e":"Since state reorganization has been on linguistic lines, religious and linguistic minorities must be considered state-wise.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"The linguistic region","b":"The State","c":"The whole of India","d":"The district"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"93rd Amendment Act","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"97th Amendment Act","b":"93rd Amendment Act","c":"86th Amendment Act","d":"99th Amendment Act"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Article 19(1)(a)","e":"The Court clarified this right cannot be exercised for commercial purposes.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Article 25","b":"Article 19(1)(a)","c":"Article 21","d":"Article 14"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Director General of Police (DGP)","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Commissioner of Police","b":"Inspector General","c":"Superintendent of Police","d":"Director General of Police (DGP)"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"Clauses (4A) and (4B)","e":"The Court held these amendments must still respect the ceiling of 50% and the concept of creamy layer, as laid down in the Indra Sawhney case (1992).","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Clauses (4A) and (4B)","b":"Clauses (1) and (2)","c":"Clauses (6) and (7)","d":"Clauses (3) and (5)"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Waman Rao case","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Waman Rao case","b":"Kesavananda Bharati case","c":"Golak Nath case","d":"Minerva Mills case"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"The concerned High Court","e":"Active euthanasia and assisted death remain illegal; the High Court Bench must seek the opinion of a committee of three doctors before deciding.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"The National Human Rights Commission","b":"The concerned High Court","c":"The Supreme Court","d":"The Central Government"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Secrecy of not casting a vote","e":"The Court held that Rules 41(2), (3) and 49-O of the Conduct of Election Rules, 1961 were ultra vires Article 19(1)(a) to the extent they violated this secrecy.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Secrecy of not casting a vote","b":"Right to a re-election","c":"Right to change their vote","d":"Right to a paper trail"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Immediately from the date of conviction","e":"The Court held Parliament lacks power under Articles 102 and 191 to create different disqualification rules for sitting members versus prospective candidates.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Only if imprisonment exceeds two years","b":"Only after the appeal period lapses","c":"Immediately from the date of conviction","d":"Only after Parliament''s approval"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"In writing and formally recorded","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"In writing and formally recorded","b":"Ratified by the Cadre Controlling Authority","c":"Reported to the CVC","d":"Approved by the Civil Service Board"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Third gender","e":"The Court held Article 21 protects the right of self-determination of gender identity, leading to the Transgender Persons (Protection of Rights) Act, 2019.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Protected minority","b":"Third gender","c":"Backward class only","d":"Scheduled community"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Information Technology Act, 2000","e":"The Court held the section was vague and over-broad, and not saved by the reasonable restrictions under Article 19(2).","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Information Technology Act, 2000","b":"Code of Criminal Procedure","c":"Telegraph Act","d":"Indian Penal Code"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"99th Amendment Act","e":"The Court also struck down the National Judicial Appointments Commission Act, 2014, restoring the earlier collegium system.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"97th Amendment Act","b":"100th Amendment Act","c":"99th Amendment Act","d":"98th Amendment Act"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Article 14","e":"The Court held this form of talaq manifestly arbitrary and also declared Section 2 of the Muslim Personal Law (Shariat) Application Act, 1937 void to that extent. This led to the Muslim Women (Protection of Rights on Marriage) Act, 2019.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Article 14","b":"Article 21","c":"Article 25","d":"Article 15"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Article 21","e":"The Court laid down a three-fold test for invasion of privacy: legality, need, and proportionality.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Article 25","b":"Article 14","c":"Article 21","d":"Article 19"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Kharak Singh case","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"A.K. Gopalan case","b":"Maneka Gandhi case","c":"Kharak Singh case","d":"Golak Nath case"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Rule 3(b) of the Kerala Hindu Places of Public Worship (Authorisation of Entry) Rules, 1965","e":"The ban was held to violate Articles 14, 21 and 25.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Rule 3(b) of the Kerala Hindu Places of Public Worship (Authorisation of Entry) Rules, 1965","b":"Section 125 of the CrPC","c":"Rule 49-O of the Conduct of Election Rules","d":"Section 377 of the IPC"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Section 497","e":"The Court also struck down Section 198(2) CrPC to the extent it applied to adultery; adultery remains a civil wrong and ground for divorce.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Section 377","b":"Section 497","c":"Section 498A","d":"Section 375"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Section 377","e":"This overruled the earlier Suresh Kumar Koushal case (2013), which had upheld Section 377''s validity.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Section 497","b":"Section 66A","c":"Section 377","d":"Section 375"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Deity Ram Lalla Virajman","e":"The Court also directed allotment of 5 acres of alternative land to the UP Sunni Central Waqf Board and overturned the 2010 Allahabad High Court judgement, which had divided the land three ways.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"The Central Government","b":"UP Sunni Central Waqf Board","c":"Deity Ram Lalla Virajman","d":"Nirmohi Akhara"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Temporary Suspension of Telecom Services (Public Emergency or Public Safety) Rules, 2017","e":"The Court held that access to the internet enjoys protection under Article 19(1)(a) and 19(1)(g), subject to the test of proportionality.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Temporary Suspension of Telecom Services (Public Emergency or Public Safety) Rules, 2017","b":"Conduct of Election Rules, 1961","c":"Right to Information Rules","d":"IT (Intermediary Guidelines) Rules"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Qualifications, achievements and merit","e":"The Court explicitly held that mere winnability could not be the basis for selection reasons; this led to the Election Commission''s Form C-7.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Financial contribution to the party","b":"Party seniority","c":"Winnability at the polls","d":"Qualifications, achievements and merit"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"Article 19(1)(g)","e":"The Court applied the test of proportionality, noting the RBI had not shown any actual loss suffered by banks due to virtual currency exchanges.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Article 19(1)(g)","b":"Article 300A","c":"Article 14","d":"Article 21"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Satwant Singh Sawhney vs. D. Ramarathnam","e":"Decided in 1967, this was one of the earliest cases expanding Article 21 beyond mere physical liberty. See table below for the full list of rights read into Article 21 through subsequent cases.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"A.K. Gopalan vs. State of Madras","b":"Satwant Singh Sawhney vs. D. Ramarathnam","c":"Kharak Singh vs. State of U.P.","d":"Maneka Gandhi vs. Union of India"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"1985","e":"This case is popularly known as the Pavement Dwellers case.","table":{"headers":["Landmark Case","Year Decided","Facet of Life or Liberty Recognised"],"rows":[["Satwant Singh Sawhney vs. D. Ramarathnam","1967","Right to travel abroad"],["Sunil Batra vs. Delhi Administration","1978","Right against solitary confinement"],["Charles Sobhraj vs. Superintendent, Central Jail","1978","Right against bar fetters"],["M.H. Hoskot vs. State of Maharashtra","1978","Right to free legal aid"],["Hussainara Khatoon vs. Home Secretary, State of Bihar","1979","Right to speedy trial"],["Prem Shankar Shukla vs. Delhi Administration","1980","Right against handcuffing"],["Kishore Singh Ravinder Dev vs. State of Rajasthan","1980","Right against inhuman police treatment"],["Francis Coralie Mullin vs. Administrator, UT of Delhi","1981","Right to live with human dignity"],["Board of Trustees of Port of Bombay vs. Dilip Kumar R. Nadkarni","1982","Right to reputation"],["T.V. Vatheeswaran vs. State of Tamil Nadu","1983","Right against delayed execution"],["Sheela Barse vs. State of Maharashtra","1983","Right against custodial violence"],["Bandhua Mukti Morcha vs. Union of India","1983","Right against bonded labour"],["Rural Litigation and Entitlement Kendra vs. State of U.P.","1985","Right to a clean and healthy environment"],["Attorney General of India vs. Lachma Devi","1985","Right against public hanging"],["Olga Tellis vs. Bombay Municipal Corporation","1985","Right to livelihood"],["State of H.P. vs. Umed Ram Sharma","1986","Right to road in hilly areas"],["Parmanand Katara vs. Union of India","1989","Right to emergency medical aid"],["Shantistar Builders vs. N.K. Totame","1990","Right to shelter"],["Unni Krishnan, J.P. vs. State of Andhra Pradesh","1993","Right to free education up to 14 years"],["Consumer Education and Research Centre vs. Union of India","1995","Right to health"],["L.I.C. of India vs. Consumer Education and Research Centre","1995","Right to an appropriate life insurance policy"],["National Human Rights Commission vs. State of Arunachal Pradesh","1996","Right not to be driven out of a state"],["Paschim Banga Khet Mazdoor Samity vs. State of West Bengal","1996","Right to timely treatment in a government hospital"],["Police Commissioner, Delhi vs. Registrar, Delhi High Court","1996","Right to fair trial"],["Vellore Citizens Welfare Forum vs. Union of India","1996","Protection against hazardous industries"],["Dr. B.L. Wadhera vs. Union of India","1996","Right to pollution-free water and air"],["Ashok Kumar Gupta vs. State of U.P.","1997","Right to social and economic justice and empowerment"],["S.K. Mastan Bee vs. G.M., South Central Railway","2002","Right to family pension"],["N.D. Jayal vs. Union of India","2003","Right to sustainable development"],["Noise Pollution, In Re.","2005","Right to freedom from noise pollution"],["Reliance Energy vs. M.S.R.D.C.","2007","Right to opportunity"],["Dilip S. Dahanukar vs. Kotak Mahindra","2007","Right of appeal from a conviction"],["Ramlila Maidan Incident, In Re.","2012","Right to sleep"],["K.S. Puttaswamy vs. Union of India","2017","Right to privacy"],["Common Cause (A Regd. Society) vs. Union of India","2018","Right to die with dignity (passive euthanasia)"],["M.K. Ranjitsinh vs. Union of India","2024","Right against adverse effects of climate change"]]},"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"1996","b":"1981","c":"1990","d":"1985"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"Supreme Court Advocates-on-Record Association vs. Union of India (2015)","e":"This is the Fourth Judges case / NJAC case. See table below for other cases that struck down constitutional amendments, wholly or in part.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Supreme Court Advocates-on-Record Association vs. Union of India (2015)","b":"Minerva Mills vs. Union of India","c":"Kesavananda Bharati vs. State of Kerala","d":"Kihoto Hollohan vs. Zachillhu"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Article 371D","e":"","table":{"headers":["Landmark Case","Constitutional Amendment at Issue","Portion Invalidated by the Court"],"rows":[["I.C. Golak Nath vs. State of Punjab (1967)","17th Amendment Act, 1964","17th Amendment (in part)"],["Kesavananda Bharati vs. State of Kerala (1973)","25th Amendment Act, 1971","Article 31C (in part)"],["Indira Nehru Gandhi vs. Raj Narain (1975)","39th Amendment Act, 1975","Article 329A, Clause (4)"],["Minerva Mills vs. Union of India (1980)","42nd Amendment Act, 1976","Article 368 (Clauses 4 & 5) and Article 31C (in part)"],["P. Sambamurthy vs. State of Andhra Pradesh (1986)","32nd Amendment Act, 1973","Article 371D, Clause (5) and its proviso"],["Kihoto Hollohan vs. Zachillhu (1992)","52nd Amendment Act, 1985","Tenth Schedule, Paragraph 7"],["L. Chandra Kumar vs. Union of India (1997)","42nd Amendment Act, 1976","Article 323A, sub-clause (d) of Clause (2), and Article 323B, sub-clause (d) of Clause (3)"],["Supreme Court Advocates-on-Record Association vs. Union of India (2015)","99th Amendment Act, 2014","99th Amendment (in total)"],["Union of India vs. Rajendra N Shah (2021)","97th Amendment Act, 2011","97th Amendment (in part)"]]},"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Article 371D","b":"Article 323A","c":"Article 356","d":"Article 32"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Romesh Thappar case and Champakam Dorairajan case","e":"A third case, Brij Bhushan vs. State of Delhi (1950), also contributed to the 1st Amendment Act. See table below for other cases that prompted specific constitutional amendments.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Golak Nath case and Sajjan Singh case","b":"Romesh Thappar case and Champakam Dorairajan case","c":"Berubari Union case and Nanavati case","d":"A.K. Gopalan case and Shankari Prasad case"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Indra Sawhney vs. Union of India","e":"","table":{"headers":["Landmark Case","Amendment That Followed"],"rows":[["Romesh Thappar vs. State of Madras (1950)","1st Amendment Act, 1951"],["Brij Bhushan vs. State of Delhi (1950)","1st Amendment Act, 1951"],["State of Madras vs. Champakam Dorairajan (1951)","1st Amendment Act, 1951"],["State of West Bengal vs. Bella Banerjee (1953)","4th Amendment Act, 1955"],["Saghir Ahmed vs. State of Uttar Pradesh (1954)","4th Amendment Act, 1955"],["State of Bombay vs. United Motors India (P) Ltd. (1953)","6th Amendment Act, 1956"],["Bengal Immunity Company Ltd. vs. State of Bihar (1955)","6th Amendment Act, 1956"],["Election Commission vs. Venkata Rao (1953)","15th Amendment Act, 1962"],["N.B. Khare vs. Election Commission (1957)","11th Amendment Act, 1961"],["Berubari Union, In Re. (1960)","9th Amendment Act, 1960"],["Chandra Mohan vs. State of U.P. (1966)","20th Amendment Act, 1962"],["I.C. Golak Nath vs. State of Punjab (1967)","24th Amendment Act, 1971"],["R.C. Cooper vs. Union of India (1970)","25th Amendment Act, 1971"],["Madhav Rao Scindia vs. Union of India (1970)","26th Amendment Act, 1971"],["Misrilal Jain vs. State of Orissa (1977)","43rd Amendment Act, 1977"],["Prabhakaran Nair vs. State of Tamil Nadu (1987)","75th Amendment Act, 1993"],["Indra Sawhney vs. Union of India (1992)","76th, 77th, 81st, 82nd and 85th Amendment Acts (1994–2001)"],["Union of India vs. Virpal Singh Chauhan (1995)","85th Amendment Act, 2001"],["S. Vinod Kumar vs. Union of India (1996)","82nd Amendment Act, 2000"],["Ajit Singh Januja vs. State of Punjab (1996)","85th Amendment Act, 2001"],["Unni Krishnan vs. State of A.P. (1993)","86th Amendment Act, 2002"],["T.M.A. Pai Foundation vs. State of Karnataka (2002)","93rd Amendment Act, 2005"],["P.A. Inamdar vs. State of Maharashtra (2005)","93rd Amendment Act, 2005"],["New India Sugar Mills vs. Commissioner of Sales Tax (1962)","46th Amendment Act, 1982"],["State of Himachal Pradesh vs. Associated Hotels of India Ltd. (1972)","46th Amendment Act, 1982"],["Oil and Natural Gas Commission vs. State of Bihar (1976)","46th Amendment Act, 1982"],["Vishu Agencies vs. Commercial Tax Officer (1977)","46th Amendment Act, 1982"],["Northern India Caterers (India) Ltd. vs. Lt. Governor of Delhi (1978)","46th Amendment Act, 1982"],["State of Madras vs. M/s. Gannon Dunkerley & Co. (1958)","46th Amendment Act, 1982"]]},"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"M. Nagaraj vs. Union of India","b":"Ajit Singh Januja vs. State of Punjab","c":"Indra Sawhney vs. Union of India","d":"T.M.A. Pai Foundation vs. State of Karnataka"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Doctrine of separability","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Doctrine of separability","b":"Doctrine of harmonious construction","c":"Doctrine of eclipse","d":"Doctrine of pith and substance"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Article 13","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Article 32","b":"Article 13","c":"Article 368","d":"Article 12"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"I and II only","e":"Under the doctrine of severability, only the offending portion of a law is declared void, not the whole of it — making statement (III) incorrect.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"II and III only","b":"I, II and III","c":"I and II only","d":"I and III only"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"The entire law is declared void","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"The law is suspended until amended","b":"The law remains valid pending judicial review","c":"Only the invalid part is struck down","d":"The entire law is declared void"},"answer":"d"}]') ON CONFLICT(chapter_slug) DO UPDATE SET subject_id=excluded.subject_id, chapter_number=excluded.chapter_number, title=excluded.title, answers_json=excluded.answers_json;
INSERT INTO chapter_content (chapter_slug, subject_id, chapter_number, title, answers_json) VALUES ('indian-polity-en-chapter-4-constitution-basics-and-preamble', 'indian-polity-en', 4, 'Constitution Basics & Preamble', '[{"a":"Constituere","e":"Constituere literally means ''to establish or set-up''.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Constituens","b":"Constitutum","c":"Constituere","d":"Constitutio"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Gilchrist","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Wade and Phillips","b":"Gettell","c":"Gilchrist","d":"Wheare"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Gettell","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Wheare","b":"Gilchrist","c":"Gettell","d":"Wade and Phillips"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Wheare","e":"The same scholar, K.C. Wheare, later classified constitutions as rigid/flexible and described the Indian Constitution as quasi-federal.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Gilchrist","b":"Wade and Phillips","c":"Wheare","d":"Gettell"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Special legal sanctity","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Federal structure","b":"Popular sovereignty","c":"Judicial supremacy","d":"Special legal sanctity"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"Elliot Bulmer","e":"Bulmer''s same framework is also the source of the procedural-prescriptive classification of constitutions.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"K.C. Wheare","b":"Elliot Bulmer","c":"Louis Henkin","d":"Lord James Bryce"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"It can abolish existing political institutions of the community","e":"Bulmer''s list states a constitution can *establish and regulate* political institutions, not abolish them.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"It can express the identity and values of a national community","b":"It can abolish existing political institutions of the community","c":"It can commit states to particular social, economic, or developmental goals","d":"It can declare and define the rights and duties of citizens"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"It can guarantee judicial review of all legislative action","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"It can declare and define the boundaries of the political community","b":"It can declare and define the nature and authority of the political community","c":"It can divide or share power between different layers of government","d":"It can guarantee judicial review of all legislative action"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"Religious identity","e":"This function specifically covers demarcating relationships between sacred and secular authorities.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Linguistic identity","b":"Religious identity","c":"Ethnic identity","d":"Cultural identity"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"I and II only","e":"The constitution is described as an ''instrument of the government'', not the judiciary.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"I, II and III","b":"I and II only","c":"I only","d":"II and III only"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Brevity, Clarity, Definiteness, Comprehensiveness, Suitability, Stability and Adaptability","e":"The seven qualities of a good constitution are:\nBrevity - precise, free of unwanted provisions.\nClarity - provisions in unambiguous terms, avoiding complicated language.\nDefiniteness - reduces judicial discretion by ensuring definite meaning.\nComprehensiveness - lays down both government powers and citizens'' rights/duties fully.\nSuitability - reflects the historical, socio-cultural, economic and political conditions of the nation.\nStability - facilitates political stability by not allowing easy tampering.\nAdaptability - dynamic rather than static, functioning as a living document.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Brevity, Clarity, Definiteness and Comprehensiveness only","b":"Suitability, Stability and Adaptability only","c":"Brevity, Clarity, Definiteness, Comprehensiveness, Suitability, Stability and Adaptability","d":"Only Comprehensiveness and Stability"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Evolved and Enacted","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Written and Unwritten","b":"Rigid and Flexible","c":"Evolved and Enacted","d":"Federal and Unitary"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Cumulative constitution","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Codified constitution","b":"Cumulative constitution","c":"Conventional constitution","d":"Documentary constitution"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"British Constitution","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"American Constitution","b":"Canadian Constitution","c":"Indian Constitution","d":"British Constitution"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"Conventional constitution","e":"Not to be confused with ''cumulative constitution'', which refers to the evolved type.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Cumulative constitution","b":"Conventional constitution","c":"Non-documentary constitution","d":"Uncodified constitution"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Written and Unwritten","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Procedural and Prescriptive","b":"Evolved and Enacted","c":"Rigid and Flexible","d":"Written and Unwritten"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"Documentary or codified constitution","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Cumulative or elastic constitution","b":"Documentary or codified constitution","c":"Non-documentary constitution","d":"Inelastic constitution"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Non-documentary or uncodified constitution","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Codified constitution","b":"Non-documentary or uncodified constitution","c":"Documentary constitution","d":"Cumulative constitution"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"I and II only","e":"UK, New Zealand and Israel are examples of unwritten constitutions, not written ones.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"I, II and III","b":"II and III only","c":"I and II only","d":"I only"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Lord James Bryce","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"K.C. Wheare","b":"Elliot Bulmer","c":"Lord James Bryce","d":"Louis Henkin"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Cannot be amended in the same manner as ordinary laws","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Makes no distinction between constitutional and ordinary law","b":"Cannot be amended at all","c":"Cannot be amended in the same manner as ordinary laws","d":"Can be amended in the same manner as ordinary laws"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Elastic constitution","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Cumulative constitution","b":"Elastic constitution","c":"Prescriptive constitution","d":"Inelastic constitution"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"USA","e":"The source lists USA, Australia and Switzerland as examples of rigid constitutions.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"UK","b":"New Zealand","c":"USA","d":"India"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"III only","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"I and III only","b":"III only","c":"II only","d":"I only"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Federal and Unitary","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Rigid and Flexible","b":"Federal and Unitary","c":"Written and Unwritten","d":"Evolved and Enacted"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Provides for a division of power between the national and regional governments","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Concentrates power in the hands of the national government","b":"Provides for a division of power between the national and regional governments","c":"Makes regional governments subordinate agencies of the national government","d":"Grants no independent jurisdiction to regional governments"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Subordinate agencies of the national government","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Autonomous federating units","b":"Co-equal partners with the national government","c":"Subordinate agencies of the national government","d":"Independent sovereign units"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Switzerland","e":"USA, Switzerland, Australia, Canada, Russia and Brazil are cited as examples of federal constitutions; UK, Japan and Italy are cited as unitary examples.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Switzerland","b":"UK","c":"Japan","d":"Italy"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"K.C. Wheare","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Lord James Bryce","b":"Louis Henkin","c":"Elliot Bulmer","d":"K.C. Wheare"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"Procedural and Prescriptive","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Rigid and Flexible","b":"Procedural and Prescriptive","c":"Federal and Unitary","d":"Evolved and Enacted"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"The legal and political structures of public institutions and legal limits of government power","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"A broad consensus on societal goals to be achieved","b":"The legal and political structures of public institutions and legal limits of government power","c":"The economic and developmental targets of the state","d":"The religious identity of the state"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"A broad consensus on common societal goals","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"A rigid amendment procedure","b":"A broad consensus on common societal goals","c":"A federal structure of governance","d":"Judicial supremacy over the legislature"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"I and II only","e":"Arbitrary power in the hands of any institution, even if conferred by a constitutional document, is a negation of constitutionalism — so a document alone does not guarantee it.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"I only","b":"I and II only","c":"II and III only","d":"I, II and III"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Limited government and the antithesis of arbitrary power","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"A system where the executive has discretionary authority over laws","b":"Limited government and the antithesis of arbitrary power","c":"A synonym for a codified constitution","d":"Unlimited government backed by a written document"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Democratic","e":"The concept holds that arbitrary power in any individual or institution''s hands, even under a constitutional document, negates constitutionalism, and constitutional government should necessarily be democratic.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Federal in structure","b":"Democratic","c":"Parliamentary in form","d":"Unitary in structure"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Conform to laws and rules under a limited government","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Hold periodic elections","b":"Conform to laws and rules under a limited government","c":"Maintain a large administrative bureaucracy","d":"Adopt a federal structure"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Friedrich","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Wheare","b":"Ybema","c":"Friedrich","d":"Roucek"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Roucek","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Friedrich","b":"Roucek","c":"Wheare","d":"Ybema"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Wheare","e":"The same scholar, K.C. Wheare, also gave the classification of constitutions as rigid and unrigid (flexible) based on amendment procedure, and described India as quasi-federal.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Ybema","b":"Wheare","c":"Friedrich","d":"Roucek"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Ybema","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Friedrich","b":"Roucek","c":"Wheare","d":"Ybema"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"I, II and III","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"II and III only","b":"I only","c":"I and II only","d":"I, II and III"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"Louis Henkin","e":"Henkin''s eight elements are:\nPopular sovereignty.\nSeparation of powers (checks and balances).\nGovernment subject to the rule of law.\nAn independent judiciary with power of judicial review.\nCivilian control of the military.\nPolice governed by law and subject to judicial control.\nDemocratic government (responsible and accountable government).\nRespect for individual rights.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Louis Henkin","b":"Lord James Bryce","c":"K.C. Wheare","d":"Elliot Bulmer"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"I and II only","e":"Henkin''s list includes \"police governed by law and judicial control,\" which is the opposite of unchecked executive control.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"II and III only","b":"I, II and III","c":"I only","d":"I and II only"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"Judicial supremacy over constitutional amendments","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"An independent judiciary","b":"Civilian control of the military","c":"Rule of law","d":"Judicial supremacy over constitutional amendments"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"The civilian authority","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"An independent commission","b":"The judiciary","c":"The police","d":"The civilian authority"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"I, II and III","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"I only","b":"I and III only","c":"I and II only","d":"I, II and III"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"42nd Amendment","e":"The 42nd Amendment Act, 1976, made sweeping changes including adding ''socialist'', ''secular'', and ''integrity'' to the Preamble and inserting Fundamental Duties.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"44th Amendment","b":"101st Amendment","c":"42nd Amendment","d":"73rd Amendment"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Kesavananda Bharati case","e":"Decided in 1973, this case established the basic structure doctrine as a limitation on Parliament''s amending power.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Golaknath case","b":"Maneka Gandhi case","c":"Kesavananda Bharati case","d":"Minerva Mills case"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"I, II and III","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"I only","b":"I, II and III","c":"II and III only","d":"I and II only"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Lengthiest written constitution","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Shortest written constitution","b":"Lengthiest written constitution","c":"Moderately sized constitution","d":"Unwritten constitution"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"395 Articles, 22 Parts","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"395 Articles, 25 Parts","b":"448 Articles, 22 Parts","c":"470 Articles, 25 Parts","d":"395 Articles, 22 Parts"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"8","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"10","b":"8","c":"22","d":"12"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"470 Articles, 25 Parts","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"470 Articles, 25 Parts","b":"500 Articles, 25 Parts","c":"448 Articles, 20 Parts","d":"395 Articles, 22 Parts"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"12","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"12","b":"14","c":"8","d":"10"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Part VII","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Part IX-A","b":"Part IV-A","c":"Part XIV-A","d":"Part VII"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"Dominance of legal luminaries in the Constituent Assembly","e":"The four factors are: geographical factors (vastness of the country and its diversity); historical factors (influence of the Government of India Act of 1935); dominance of legal luminaries in the Constituent Assembly; and a single Constitution for both the Centre and the states (not multiple separate constitutions).","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Dominance of legal luminaries in the Constituent Assembly","b":"Absence of any foreign constitutional influence","c":"Short duration of Constituent Assembly deliberations","d":"Exclusion of administrative provisions from the document"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Article 370","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Article 370","b":"Article 375","c":"Article 371","d":"Article 356"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"The Constitution (Application to Jammu and Kashmir) Order, 2019","e":"This 2019 order superseded the earlier 1954 order of the same broader title and extended all provisions of the Constitution of India to Jammu and Kashmir.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"The Constitution (Application to Jammu and Kashmir) Order, 1954","b":"The Jammu and Kashmir Reorganisation Order, 2019","c":"The Constitution (Application to Jammu and Kashmir) Order, 2019","d":"The Jammu and Kashmir Special Status Repeal Order, 2019"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Jammu & Kashmir and Ladakh","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Ladakh and Gilgit","b":"Jammu & Kashmir and Ladakh","c":"Kashmir and Jammu","d":"Jammu and Baltistan"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Dr. B.R. Ambedkar","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Jawaharlal Nehru","b":"Dr. B.R. Ambedkar","c":"Sardar Patel","d":"Rajendra Prasad"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"I, II and III","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"I only","b":"II and III only","c":"I, II and III","d":"I and II only"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Government of India Act, 1935","e":"The Federal Scheme, Judiciary, Governors, Emergency Powers, Public Service Commissions, and most administrative details are drawn from this Act, with more than half of the Constitution''s provisions closely resembling it.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"British Constitution","b":"US Constitution","c":"Government of India Act, 1935","d":"Irish Constitution"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Method of amendment","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Written or unwritten form","b":"Method of amendment","c":"Length of the document","d":"Federal or unitary structure"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"American Constitution","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Israeli Constitution","b":"New Zealand Constitution","c":"American Constitution","d":"British Constitution"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Ratification by half of the total states","e":"This applies to provisions affecting the federal structure, such as election of the President and distribution of legislative powers.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Approval of the President alone","b":"Ratification by half of the total states","c":"Ratification by all the states","d":"A national referendum"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Special majority amendment","e":"A third category — provisions amendable by a simple majority in the manner of ordinary legislative process — also exists and falls outside the scope of Article 368.\n\n**StudyHelp – TSPSC Polity MCQs — Ch.4 Salient Features of the Constitution — Batch 2 (Features 4-7)**","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Amendment by ordinary legislative process","b":"Special majority with state ratification","c":"Special majority amendment","d":"Simple majority amendment"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Federal system with unitary bias","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Federal system with unitary bias","b":"Confederal system","c":"Purely unitary system","d":"Purely federal system"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"I and II only","e":"Single citizenship is actually one of the unitary (non-federal) features present in the Indian Constitution, not a federal feature.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"I and II only","b":"I only","c":"I, II and III","d":"II and III only"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Appointment of state governor by the Centre","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Rigidity of Constitution","b":"Bicameralism","c":"Appointment of state governor by the Centre","d":"Independent judiciary"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Article 1","e":"Article 1 describes India as a ''Union of States'', a term nowhere replaced by the word ''Federation'' anywhere in the Constitution.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Article 5","b":"Article 370","c":"Article 1","d":"Article 3"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Union of States","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Federation of States","b":"Union of States","c":"Confederation of States","d":"Republic of States"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"I and II only","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"I, II and III","b":"II and III only","c":"I and II only","d":"I only"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"K.C. Wheare","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Ivor Jennings","b":"K.C. Wheare","c":"Granville Austin","d":"Morris Jones"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Morris Jones","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"K.C. Wheare","b":"Morris Jones","c":"Granville Austin","d":"Ivor Jennings"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"I and II only","e":"''Bargaining federalism'' was coined by Morris Jones, not K.C. Wheare, who instead described the Constitution as ''quasi-federal''.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"I only","b":"II and III only","c":"I, II and III","d":"I and II only"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"American Presidential System","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"American Presidential System","b":"French Semi-Presidential System","c":"Swiss Directorial System","d":"Chinese One-Party System"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Co-operation and co-ordination","e":"In contrast, the presidential system is based on the doctrine of separation of powers between the two organs.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Co-operation and co-ordination","b":"Strict separation of powers","c":"Judicial oversight of the executive","d":"Complete independence of the executive"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Westminster Model","e":"Westminster is the place in London where the British Parliament is located, and the term is often used as a symbol or synonym for it.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Cabinet-less Model","b":"Federal Model","c":"Westminster Model","d":"Directorial Model"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"I and II only","e":"Dissolution of the lower House (Lok Sabha or Assembly) is itself one of the listed features of parliamentary government, contradicting the idea of a fixed tenure with no dissolution.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"I only","b":"I and II only","c":"II and III only","d":"I, II and III"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"The Indian Parliament is not a sovereign body unlike the British Parliament","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Britain has a bicameral legislature while India does not","b":"India has a nominal executive while Britain does not","c":"India follows collective responsibility while Britain does not","d":"The Indian Parliament is not a sovereign body unlike the British Parliament"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"India has an elected head while Britain has a hereditary head","e":"India is a republic, while Britain remains a monarchy.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Both have an elected head","b":"Both have a hereditary head","c":"India has a hereditary head while Britain has an elected head","d":"India has an elected head while Britain has a hereditary head"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"The Prime Minister","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"The Chief Justice","b":"The Speaker","c":"The Prime Minister","d":"The President or Monarch"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Britain","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"USA","b":"Australia","c":"Britain","d":"India"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"American Supreme Court","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"British Parliament","b":"Indian Parliament","c":"American Supreme Court","d":"Indian Supreme Court"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Due process of law versus procedure established by law","e":"The American Constitution provides for ''due process of law'', while the Indian Constitution under Article 21 provides for ''procedure established by law'', which is narrower in scope.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Judicial supremacy versus parliamentary sovereignty","b":"Due process of law versus procedure established by law","c":"Basic structure versus constituent power","d":"Rule of law versus due process"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Article 21","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Article 14","b":"Article 32","c":"Article 19","d":"Article 21"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"British parliamentary sovereignty and American judicial supremacy","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Federalism and unitarism","b":"British parliamentary sovereignty and American judicial supremacy","c":"Rigidity and flexibility","d":"Fundamental Rights and Directive Principles"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Power of judicial review","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Power of judicial review","b":"Advisory jurisdiction","c":"Constituent power","d":"Contempt power"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Integrated as well as independent","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Independent but not integrated","b":"Integrated but not independent","c":"Neither integrated nor independent","d":"Integrated as well as independent"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"A single integrated system of courts headed by the Supreme Court","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Special tribunals independent of the Supreme Court","b":"Two parallel and separate systems of courts","c":"State-level courts alone, with no central oversight","d":"A single integrated system of courts headed by the Supreme Court"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"I and II only","e":"There is actually a prohibition on discussion of the conduct of judges in the legislatures, which is itself one of the safeguards ensuring judicial independence.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"II and III only","b":"I and II only","c":"I, II and III","d":"I only"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Six","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Eight","b":"Six","c":"Five","d":"Seven"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Articles 14-18","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Articles 23-24","b":"Articles 19-22","c":"Articles 25-28","d":"Articles 14-18"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"Articles 19-22","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Articles 14-18","b":"Articles 19-22","c":"Articles 23-24","d":"Articles 29-30"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Articles 23-24","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Articles 19-22","b":"Articles 23-24","c":"Articles 25-28","d":"Articles 29-30"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Articles 25-28","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Articles 29-30","b":"Articles 25-28","c":"Articles 23-24","d":"Articles 19-22"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Article 32","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Article 44","b":"Article 21","c":"Article 32","d":"Article 30"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Seven","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Seven","b":"Eight","c":"Five","d":"Six"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Right to Property","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Right to Freedom of Religion","b":"Right to Property","c":"Right against Exploitation","d":"Right to Equality"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Article 300-A","