ticle 49","c":"Article 50","d":"Article 48"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Article 50","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Article 49","b":"Article 48A","c":"Article 50","d":"Article 51"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Article 51","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Article 48A","b":"Article 50","c":"Article 49","d":"Article 51"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"14 years","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"10 years","b":"6 years","c":"18 years","d":"14 years"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"I, II and III","e":"The 42nd Amendment Act, 1976 added four new Directive Principles: Article 39 (healthy development of children), Article 39A (free legal aid), Article 43A (worker participation in industry management), and Article 48A (environment and wildlife protection).","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"I, II and III","b":"I and II only","c":"II and III only","d":"I only"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Sir B.N. Rau","e":"Sir B.N. Rau was the Constitutional Advisor to the Constituent Assembly. Following his recommendation, justiciable rights were placed in Part III (Fundamental Rights) and non-justiciable rights in Part IV (Directive Principles).","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"K.M. Munshi","b":"Sir B.N. Rau","c":"Dr. B.R. Ambedkar","d":"Alladi Krishnaswamy Ayyar"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"I and II only","e":"Statement III is reversed — Fundamental Rights do not require legislation and are automatically enforced, while Directive Principles require legislation and are not automatically enforced.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"II and III only","b":"I and II only","c":"I only","d":"I, II and III"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Political democracy","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Participatory democracy","b":"Judicial democracy","c":"Federal democracy","d":"Political democracy"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"Fundamental Rights have legal sanctions; Directive Principles have moral and political sanctions","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Both have only moral sanctions","b":"Fundamental Rights have moral sanctions; Directive Principles have legal sanctions","c":"Both have only legal sanctions","d":"Fundamental Rights have legal sanctions; Directive Principles have moral and political sanctions"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"Cannot declare it unconstitutional, though they can uphold a law enacted to give effect to a directive","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Cannot declare it unconstitutional, though they can uphold a law enacted to give effect to a directive","b":"Declare it unconstitutional and invalid","c":"Refer it to the President","d":"Suspend the law pending review"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"K.T. Shah","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"T.T. Krishnamachari","b":"Sir Ivor Jennings","c":"N. Srinivasan","d":"K.T. Shah"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"T.T. Krishnamachari","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"K.C. Wheare","b":"Nasiruddin","c":"T.T. Krishnamachari","d":"K.T. Shah"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Sir Ivor Jennings","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"N. Srinivasan","b":"K. Santhanam","c":"Sir Ivor Jennings","d":"K.C. Wheare"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"All of the above","e":"K. Santhanam pointed to potential conflict between the Centre and the states, between the President and the Prime Minister, and between the Governor and the Chief Minister over implementation of the Directive Principles.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"All of the above","b":"Centre and states only","c":"President and Prime Minister only","d":"Governor and Chief Minister only"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Nasiruddin","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Nasiruddin","b":"N. Srinivasan","c":"Sir Ivor Jennings","d":"K.T. Shah"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"N. Srinivasan","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"K. Santhanam","b":"K.C. Wheare","c":"T.T. Krishnamachari","d":"N. Srinivasan"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"L.M. Singhvi","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"L.M. Singhvi","b":"M.C. Setalvad","c":"Granville Austin","d":"M.C. Chagla"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"M.C. Chagla","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"M.C. Setalvad","b":"P.B. Gajendragadkar","c":"L.M. Singhvi","d":"M.C. Chagla"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"Economic democracy","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Social democracy","b":"Judicial democracy","c":"Participatory democracy","d":"Economic democracy"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"The social revolution","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Judicial reform","b":"The social revolution","c":"Federal integration","d":"Economic liberalisation"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Power of judicial review","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Power of judicial review","b":"Power of advisory jurisdiction","c":"Power of original jurisdiction","d":"Power of contempt"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Fundamental Rights","e":"The Court held that Directive Principles must conform to and run as subsidiary to Fundamental Rights, but Fundamental Rights could be amended by Parliament through constitutional amendment acts. Following this ruling, Parliament made the 1st, 4th, and 17th Amendment Acts to implement some of the Directive Principles.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Directive Principles","b":"Fundamental Rights","c":"Neither, Parliament decides","d":"The President''s discretion applies"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Sacrosanct","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Subsidiary","b":"Conditional","c":"Sacrosanct","d":"Absolute"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"24th Amendment Act","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"44th Amendment Act","b":"24th Amendment Act","c":"42nd Amendment Act","d":"25th Amendment Act"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Article 31C","e":"As originally inserted, Article 31C protected laws implementing the Directive Principles specified in Article 39(b) and (c).","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Article 31B","b":"Article 31C","c":"Article 31D","d":"Article 31A"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"The provision barring courts from questioning laws that claim to give effect to Article 39","e":"The Court held this second provision unconstitutional on the ground that judicial review is a basic feature of the Constitution. However, the first provision of Article 31C was upheld as valid.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"The provision extending protection to all Directive Principles","b":"The definition of ''State'' under Article 31C","c":"The provision barring courts from questioning laws that claim to give effect to Article 39","d":"The provision immunising laws from Article 14 and 19 challenges"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Any of the Directive Principles","e":"This extension, which accorded legal primacy to Directive Principles over Fundamental Rights, was declared unconstitutional in the Minerva Mills case (1980). The Minerva Mills ruling restored the subordination of Directive Principles to Fundamental Rights, though Fundamental Rights under Articles 14 and 19 remained subordinate specifically to Article 39(b) and (c).","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Only Article 39","b":"Any of the Directive Principles","c":"Only Gandhian Directive Principles","d":"Only socialistic Directive Principles"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"44th Amendment Act, 1978","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"44th Amendment Act, 1978","b":"42nd Amendment Act, 1976","c":"25th Amendment Act, 1971","d":"86th Amendment Act, 2002"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Two wheels of a chariot","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Two wheels of a chariot","b":"Two sides of a coin","c":"Two pillars of a temple","d":"Two branches of a tree"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Fundamental Rights enjoy supremacy over Directive Principles","e":"Even so, Parliament can amend Fundamental Rights to implement Directive Principles, provided the amendment does not damage or destroy the basic structure of the Constitution.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Parliament alone decides supremacy case by case","b":"Fundamental Rights enjoy supremacy over Directive Principles","c":"Both are equal and cannot be balanced","d":"Directive Principles enjoy supremacy over Fundamental Rights"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"I and II only","e":"The Golaknath judgment held the opposite of statement III — that Fundamental Rights could NOT be amended for implementing Directive Principles. This position was later reversed by the 24th Amendment Act.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"I and II only","b":"I, II and III","c":"II and III only","d":"I and III only"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Planning Commission","e":"The Planning Commission was replaced by NITI Aayog in 2015.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Planning Commission","b":"Finance Commission","c":"National Development Council","d":"Election Commission"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Legal Services Authorities Act, 1987","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Consumer Protection Act, 1986","b":"Right to Information Act, 2005","c":"Legal Services Authorities Act, 1987","d":"Protection of Civil Rights Act, 1976"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"A civil court","e":"Lok adalat awards are enforceable, binding on the parties, and final, with no appeal lying before any court against them.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"An arbitration panel","b":"An administrative body","c":"A tribunal","d":"A civil court"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"73rd Amendment Act","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"73rd Amendment Act","b":"42nd Amendment Act","c":"86th Amendment Act","d":"65th Amendment Act"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"National Commission for Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes","e":"This combined commission was later bifurcated into two separate bodies — the National Commission for Scheduled Castes and the National Commission for Scheduled Tribes — by the 89th Amendment Act, 2003.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"National Human Rights Commission","b":"National Commission for Backward Classes","c":"National Commission for Minorities","d":"National Commission for Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"102nd Amendment Act, 2018","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"97th Amendment Act, 2011","b":"103rd Amendment Act, 2019","c":"102nd Amendment Act, 2018","d":"89th Amendment Act, 2003"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Economically Weaker Sections (EWS)","e":"This Amendment enabled 10% reservation in admissions to educational institutions and civil posts for EWS persons not already covered under existing SC/ST/OBC reservation schemes.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Scheduled Tribes","b":"Economically Weaker Sections (EWS)","c":"Other Backward Classes","d":"Scheduled Castes"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Criminal Procedure Code, 1973","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Indian Penal Code, 1860","b":"Civil Procedure Code, 1908","c":"Criminal Procedure Code, 1973","d":"Evidence Act, 1872"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Ancient and Historical Monument and Archaeological Sites and Remains Act, 1951","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Antiquities and Art Treasures Act, 1972","b":"Ancient and Historical Monument and Archaeological Sites and Remains Act, 1951","c":"Forest Conservation Act, 1980","d":"National Heritage Act, 1958"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Article 335","e":"Article 335 is located in Part XVI of the Constitution and, though non-justiciable like other Directive Principles, is given equal importance by the judiciary.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Article 350-A","b":"Article 335","c":"Article 351","d":"Article 340"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Article 350-A","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Article 335","b":"Article 351","c":"Article 350-A","d":"Article 29"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Article 351","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Article 343","b":"Article 335","c":"Article 350-A","d":"Article 351"},"answer":"d"}]') ON CONFLICT(chapter_slug) DO UPDATE SET subject_id=excluded.subject_id, chapter_number=excluded.chapter_number, title=excluded.title, answers_json=excluded.answers_json;
INSERT INTO chapter_content (chapter_slug, subject_id, chapter_number, title, answers_json) VALUES ('indian-polity-en-chapter-8-fundamental-duties', 'indian-polity-en', 8, 'Fundamental Duties', '[{"a":"Fundamental Rights only","e":"The framers incorporated the duties of the State as Directive Principles of State Policy, but did not include duties of citizens.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Fundamental Rights only","b":"Fundamental Duties only","c":"Both Fundamental Rights and Fundamental Duties","d":"Neither"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"1976","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"1971","b":"1976","c":"1978","d":"1980"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"2002","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"1992","b":"2002","c":"2005","d":"2011"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Erstwhile USSR","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Japan","b":"France","c":"Erstwhile USSR","d":"Germany"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Japan","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Canada","b":"Australia","c":"USA","d":"Japan"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"1976","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"1971","b":"1975","c":"1976","d":"1978"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"The internal emergency (1975–1977)","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"The Indo-Pakistani War of 1971","b":"The internal emergency (1975–1977)","c":"The Sino-Indian War of 1962","d":"The linguistic reorganisation of states"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"42nd Amendment Act, 1976","e":"This Amendment added a new Part IVA to the Constitution.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"44th Amendment Act","b":"42nd Amendment Act, 1976","c":"24th Amendment Act","d":"86th Amendment Act"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Part IV-A","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Part III-A","b":"Part IV-A","c":"Part IX-A","d":"Part XIV-A"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Only one","e":"Part IVA consists of a single Article, Article 51A.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Two","b":"Three","c":"Only one","d":"Five"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"I, II and III","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"I only","b":"I and II only","c":"II and III only","d":"I, II and III"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"I, II and III","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"I only","b":"I and II only","c":"II and III only","d":"I, II and III"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"To abide by the Constitution and respect its ideals and institutions, the National Flag and the National Anthem","e":"Paying taxes, casting a vote, and family planning were all cited by critics as important duties left out of the list.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"To pay taxes honestly","b":"To cast one''s vote in elections","c":"To abide by the Constitution and respect its ideals and institutions, the National Flag and the National Anthem","d":"To practise family planning"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"To renounce practices derogatory to the dignity of women","e":"This forms part of the duty to promote harmony and the spirit of common brotherhood amongst all people of India.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"To renounce practices derogatory to the dignity of women","b":"To pay income tax regularly","c":"To participate in local self-government","d":"To undergo compulsory military training"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Article 51A","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Article 51","b":"Article 21A","c":"Article 51A","d":"Article 45"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Duty to provide opportunities for education to one''s child or ward between six and fourteen years","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Duty to protect and improve the natural environment","b":"Duty to develop scientific temper","c":"Duty to provide opportunities for education to one''s child or ward between six and fourteen years","d":"Duty to safeguard public property"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Eleven","e":"","table":{"headers":["No.","Fundamental Duty"],"rows":[["1","Abide by the Constitution and respect its ideals and institutions, the National Flag and the National Anthem"],["2","Cherish and follow the noble ideals that inspired the national struggle for freedom"],["3","Uphold and protect the sovereignty, unity and integrity of India"],["4","Defend the country and render national service when called upon to do so"],["5","Promote harmony and the spirit of common brotherhood amongst all the people of India, transcending religious, linguistic and regional or sectional diversities, and to renounce practices derogatory to the dignity of women"],["6","Value and preserve the rich heritage of the country''s composite culture"],["7","Protect and improve the natural environment including forests, lakes, rivers and wildlife, and have compassion for living creatures"],["8","Develop scientific temper, humanism and the spirit of inquiry and reform"],["9","Safeguard public property and abjure violence"],["10","Strive towards excellence in all spheres of individual and collective activity so that the nation constantly rises to higher levels of endeavour and achievement"],["11","Provide opportunities for education to one''s child or ward between the age of six and fourteen years (added by the 86th Amendment Act, 2002)"]]},"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Eight","b":"Nine","c":"Ten","d":"Eleven"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"I, II and III","e":"Cherishing noble ideals of the freedom struggle is a moral precept, while respecting the Constitution, National Flag and National Anthem is a civic duty.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"I only","b":"I and II only","c":"II and III only","d":"I, II and III"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"Article 21","e":"Articles 14, 20, 21, 21A, 22, 23, 24, 25, 26, 27 and 28 are available to all persons, whether citizens or foreigners; Fundamental Duties, by contrast, are confined to citizens only.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Article 15","b":"Article 19","c":"Article 21","d":"Article 30"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Non-justiciable","e":"There is no direct legal sanction against their violation, though Parliament is free to enforce them by suitable legislation.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Justiciable","b":"Non-justiciable","c":"Enforceable only during a National Emergency","d":"Enforceable only against the State"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Casting vote, paying taxes and family planning","e":"The duty to pay taxes had in fact been recommended by the Swaran Singh Committee but was not accepted.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Casting vote, paying taxes and family planning","b":"Respecting the National Flag","c":"Protecting the environment","d":"Safeguarding public property"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"D.D. Chawla","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"C.K. Daphtary","b":"A.K. Sen","c":"D.D. Chawla","d":"H.R. Gokhale"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"C.K. Daphtary","e":"Daphtary was the former Attorney General of India.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"D.D. Chawla","b":"C.K. Daphtary","c":"H.R. Gokhale","d":"Indira Gandhi"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Part IV","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Part III","b":"Part IV","c":"Part V","d":"Part IX"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Mohan Kumar Singhania case","e":"Mohan Kumar Singhania vs. Union of India (1991).","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Ramlila Maidan Incident case","b":"Minerva Mills case","c":"Mohan Kumar Singhania case","d":"Kesavananda Bharati case"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Parts III, IV and IVA","e":"The Court noted that one Part enumerates Fundamental Rights, the second declares Directive Principles of governance, and the third lays down Fundamental Duties.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Parts III, IV and IVA","b":"Parts I, II and III","c":"Parts IV, V and VI","d":"Parts XIV, XIVA and XV"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Appropriate penalty or punishment","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Suspension of citizenship","b":"Appropriate penalty or punishment","c":"Loss of voting rights","d":"Compulsory community service"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"H.R. Gokhale","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"H.R. Gokhale","b":"C.K. Daphtary","c":"A.K. Sen","d":"Morarji Desai"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Indira Gandhi","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Morarji Desai","b":"Indira Gandhi","c":"Rajiv Gandhi","d":"Jawaharlal Nehru"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"The Janata Government headed by Morarji Desai","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Indira Gandhi''s Congress Government","b":"Rajiv Gandhi''s Government","c":"The Janata Government headed by Morarji Desai","d":"The V.P. Singh Government"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"43rd and 44th Amendment Acts","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"43rd and 44th Amendment Acts","b":"44th and 52nd Amendment Acts","c":"24th and 25th Amendment Acts","d":"42nd and 86th Amendment Acts"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"1999","e":"","table":{"headers":["Act","Provision relevant to Fundamental Duties"],"rows":[["Prevention of Insults to National Honour Act, 1971","Prevents disrespect to the Constitution, National Flag and National Anthem"],["Various criminal laws","Punish encouraging enmity between sections of people on grounds of language, race, place of birth, religion, etc."],["Protection of Civil Rights Act, 1955","Punishes offences related to caste and religion (known as the Untouchability (Offences) Act till 1976)"],["Indian Penal Code (IPC)","Declares imputations/assertions prejudicial to national integration as punishable offences"],["Unlawful Activities (Prevention) Act, 1967","Provides for declaring a communal organisation an unlawful association"],["Representation of the People Act, 1951","Disqualifies MPs/MLAs for corrupt practices such as soliciting votes on religious/caste/communal grounds"],["Wildlife (Protection) Act, 1972","Prohibits trade in rare and endangered species"],["Forest (Conservation) Act, 1980","Checks indiscriminate deforestation and diversion of forest land"]]},"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"1990","b":"1993","c":"1999","d":"2003"},"answer":"c"}]') ON CONFLICT(chapter_slug) DO UPDATE SET subject_id=excluded.subject_id, chapter_number=excluded.chapter_number, title=excluded.title, answers_json=excluded.answers_json;
INSERT INTO chapter_content (chapter_slug, subject_id, chapter_number, title, answers_json) VALUES ('indian-polity-en-chapter-9-constitution-and-amendments', 'indian-polity-en', 9, 'Basic Structure of Constitution and Amendments', '[{"a":"Shankari Prasad case","e":"The Shankari Prasad case (1951) challenged the validity of the First Amendment Act, which had curtailed the right to property, prompting this first challenge to Parliament''s amending power under Article 368.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Golak Nath case","b":"Shankari Prasad case","c":"Kesavananda Bharati case","d":"Sajjan Singh case"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Constitutional amendment acts","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Constitutional amendment acts","b":"Delegated legislation","c":"Executive orders","d":"Ordinary legislation"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Sajjan Singh case","e":"The Sajjan Singh case was decided in 1964.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Sajjan Singh case","b":"Minerva Mills case","c":"Golak Nath case","d":"Waman Rao case"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Seventeenth Amendment Act","e":"This Amendment Act (1964) had inserted certain state acts into the Ninth Schedule. In this case, the Supreme Court held that Fundamental Rights occupy a transcendental and immutable position.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Seventeenth Amendment Act","b":"39th Amendment Act","c":"24th Amendment Act","d":"First Amendment Act"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"24th Amendment Act","e":"The 24th Amendment Act was enacted in 1971, amending Articles 13 and 368. It also made it obligatory for the President to give assent to a constitutional amendment bill — before this Act, the President could withhold assent or return the bill for reconsideration.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"39th Amendment Act","b":"44th Amendment Act","c":"24th Amendment Act","d":"42nd Amendment Act"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Golak Nath case","e":"The Kesavananda Bharati case upheld the validity of the 24th Amendment Act, and laid down the doctrine of basic structure.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Golak Nath case","b":"Sajjan Singh case","c":"Minerva Mills case","d":"Shankari Prasad case"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"Indira Nehru Gandhi case","e":"This case was decided in 1975. It invalidated a provision of the 39th Amendment Act, which had kept election disputes involving the Prime Minister and the Speaker of Lok Sabha outside the jurisdiction of all courts.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Indira Nehru Gandhi case","b":"S.R. Bommai case","c":"Waman Rao case","d":"Minerva Mills case"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"42nd Amendment Act","e":"The 42nd Amendment Act was enacted in 1976. It amended Article 368 to declare Parliament''s constituent power unlimited.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"39th Amendment Act","b":"44th Amendment Act","c":"42nd Amendment Act","d":"24th Amendment Act"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Judicial review","e":"The Minerva Mills case was decided in 1980.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Federal character","b":"Judicial review","c":"Rule of law","d":"Free and fair elections"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"24 April 1973","e":"This date corresponds to the judgement in the Kesavananda Bharati case.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"26 January 1950","b":"1 April 1980","c":"25 June 1975","d":"24 April 1973"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"I and II only","e":"The 24th Amendment Act was enacted after Golak Nath in reaction to it, and its validity was upheld later in the Kesavananda Bharati case.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"I and II only","b":"I, II and III","c":"II and III only","d":"I only"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"I and II only","e":"The Minerva Mills case was decided in 1980, not 1976.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"II and III only","b":"I, II and III","c":"I only","d":"I and II only"},"answer":"d"},{"a":"Articles 32, 136, 141 and 142","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Articles 136, 141, 226 and 227","b":"Articles 32, 136, 141 and 142","c":"Articles 32, 226, 227 and 368","d":"Articles 14, 19, 21 and 32"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Articles 226 and 227","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Articles 226 and 227","b":"Articles 245 and 246","c":"Articles 32 and 136","d":"Articles 141 and 142"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"I and II only","e":"Single citizenship is not listed among the elements of the basic structure; federal and secular character are.","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"I, II and III","b":"II and III only","c":"I and II only","d":"I only"},"answer":"c"},{"a":"Uniform Civil Code","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Judicial review","b":"Uniform Civil Code","c":"Rule of law","d":"Parliamentary system"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Doctrine of basic structure","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Doctrine of colourable legislation","b":"Doctrine of basic structure","c":"Doctrine of pith and substance","d":"Doctrine of severability"},"answer":"b"},{"a":"Central Coal Fields Ltd. case","e":"","table":null,"type":"mcq","options":{"a":"Central Coal Fields Ltd. case","b":"Bhim Singhji case","c":"Delhi Judicial Service Association case","d":"All India Judge''s Association case"},"answer":"a"},{"a":"I and II only","e":"The Namit Sharma case (2012) declared \"freedom and dignity of the individual,\" not judicial review.","table":{"headers":["Landmark Case (Year)","Basic Structure Elements Recognised"],"rows":[["Kesavananda Bharati (1973)","Constitutional supremacy, power separation, democratic-republican form, secularism, federalism, national sovereignty and unity, individual liberty and dignity, welfare-state goal, parliamentary form"],["Indira Nehru Gandhi (1975)","Democratic-republican sovereignty, equal status and opportunity, religious freedom and secular character, governance by law rather than by individuals, judicial review, fair electoral process"],["Minerva Mills (1980)","Restricted scope of Parliament''s amending power, judicial review, equilibrium bet